  "verse": 39,
  "plain_english": "Dyczkowski-mode.*\n\n**Grace, initiation, and the one liberated while living**\n\nAll that has been established — the valid means, its fruit, the primacy of the knower — now comes to\nits practical point. How does one actually attain the Lord? The whole vimarśa closes with the answer:\nby the Lord's grace, working through a fourfold effort, and culminating in the one liberated while\nstill living.\n\nThere is a supreme work to be accomplished. Even if it seems impossible in itself, it is undertaken\nas possible; and the effort that drives it is the life of the fourfold work. But the decisive factor is\nnot the effort alone — it is the *grace* of the Lord. Only when the Lord's self is pleased, when the\nturbidity of our conceit in the body and the rest is abandoned, are we able to cross the great ocean\nof delusion. The means are laid out in the Lord-taught teaching: the initiatory rite (dīkṣā), the\nvision-yoga (dhyāna), knowledge (jñāna), and devotion (bhāvanā). By their combined capacity, the\naspirant abandons the world that is to be left behind — the knower-made world of body, breath, the\neight-fold city and the void, the world of the things that support and objectify enjoyment — and,\nthrough the grace of the Lord and the guru, brings the lordship itself to manifestation within his own\nself.\n\nThe order matters. Although vision and knowledge are primary, knowledge is preceded by initiation. As\nthe teaching says, there is no eligibility for the yoga of Śaṅkara without the dīkṣā; and the dīkṣā is\ntwofold, by rite and by knowledge. But what if one, assured by the knowledge-initiation alone, has not\npracticed the vision-yoga? Then there is devotion (bhāvanā). Some, the yogins, attain a share of the\nwondrous powers — a portion of all-knowing and all-doing, the eight-fold vibhūti, pertaining to this\nor that world. But even where such powers are manifest, nothing of the present goal is lost; and this,\ntoo, is established by reason.\n\nNow a subtle point about the self. The prāṇa — the vital breath, the body, the rest — is experienced\nas \"I.\" How is this compatible with the param-ātman, the supreme self? There is no conflict, because\nthe two are present together. The prāṇa's being felt as \"I\" is nothing other than the supreme self's\nown free manifestation: the param-ātman shines freely, and by that very shining the prāṇa and the\nrest appear as \"I.\" Even the knower-ness that touches them is that same light. The object is\nremembered, because experience is broken and re-collected; but the knower — whose essence is the\nun-broken light — is not something that could be remembered. As the Spandakārikā says, remembered-ness\nis not how the essence is realized. So the Lord-ness is not a new thing to be proved in the\nconsciousness-essence; and the negation of \"I\" in the breath and the body is not a new discovery\neither. Both were there from the beginning.\n\nAnd so the end. In all this there is the utter extinction of the delusion-impression. And when that\nis extinguished, when the restraint of the body-and-\"I\" conceit dissolves, there is the Lord-ness\nitself. Yet even here, by the Lord's will alone, for some the recognition does not arise despite a\nhundred efforts. But even for such a one, merely by coming to hear the teaching, by the ordered\nripening of the impression, there is necessarily the attainment of one's own form. The āgama declares:\n\"Unwavering in self-experience, he is called the jīvanmukta, the one liberated while living; but he\nwho still constructs the essence — at the fall of the body, he goes to Śiva.\" And Abhinavagupta adds\nhis own verse: the sparks of the pure-knowledge-fire blaze when the inner intent is ended, like the\nroot-leaves of the old tree of saṃsāra caught in the fire of the supreme reality.\n\nHence, by all this, **nothing new is established in the self**. The Lord was always the own self;\ngrace, initiation, vision, knowledge and devotion only reveal what was already there. This, Abhinavagupta\nsays, is what was declared at the very beginning of the teaching — in the agent, the knower, the own\nself, the ādi-siddha, the already-established. The tṛtīyo vimarśa — the reflexive-awareness of the\nvalid means, its fruit, and the supreme truth of the known — here comes to its close.\n\n- **Preserved:** the supreme work assumed as possible; the fourfold work; only-by-grace; the\n  caturvidha kṛtya; dīkṣā/dhyāna/jñāna/bhāvanā; the abandoning of the heya-world; grace of Lord and\n  guru; the dīkṣā-priority (mā. vi. 4.6); the jñāna-dīkṣā/bhāvanā; the vibhūti-share of the yogins;\n  the prāṇa/ātma and the param-ātman present together; the prāṇa's \"I\" as the param-ātman's free\n  manifestation; the knower un-remembered (spa. kā. 13); Lord-ness not new; the delusion-impression\n  extinction; the jīvanmukta (āgama verse + Abhinava's own); nothing new established (1.1.2); the\n  ||3|| colophon.\n- **Added (SUPPLIED, recoverable):** \"the order matters\" (glosses the dīkṣā-priority); \"they only\n  reveal what was already there\" (states the \"nothing new\" point); \"the jīvanmukta, the one liberated\n  while living\" (gives the term). Interpretive unpacking, not new doctrine.\n- **NOT resolved:** the canonical enumeration of the fourfold kṛtya; the exact ontology of the\n  \"mohakalāsaṃskāra\" (delusion-impression). Logged, not invented.\n\n**Verdict:** understood and readable. The tṛtīyo vimarśa closes cleanly: attainment is by grace\nthrough the fourfold effort (initiation first), the prāṇa's \"I\" is the supreme self's free\nmanifestation, the knower is un-broken and un-remembered, and on the extinction of the delusion-\nimpression the Lord-ness itself arises — the jīvanmukta, in whom nothing new is established.",
  "sanskrit_cite": "pārameśvaraṃ | kimapi | kṛtyaṃ | nirvāhaṇīyamiti | so'saṃbhavannapi | saṃbhāvya | eva | | | prayatna | iti | caturvidhasya | kṛtyasya",
